      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "s4er",
          "text": "What Is a Side-Impact Accident?",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "b4eu",
      "style": "normal",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "s4et",
          "text": "A side-impact accident occurs when the front of one vehicle crashes into the side of another. It is also referred to as a broadside or T-bone accident because the two vehicles form the shape of a “T.” These accidents can be severe since the point of impact is close to where the driver or passenger of a vehicle sits. There is no crumple zone to act as a barrier between the vehicle’s occupants and the force of impact.",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "b4ew",
      "style": "h2",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "s4ev",
          "text": "Common Injuries From Side-Impact Accidents",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "b4ey",
      "style": "normal",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "s4ex",
          "text": "Side-impact or broadside accidents can be catastrophic in the amount of damage they inflict on those involved. They have a high risk of injury, especially for the passengers sitting on the side of the car struck. If there is speeding involved or a significant size difference between the two vehicles, this can also increase the level of damage.",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
